**Vanta — Vendor Risk Assessment: 'CloudSync Technologies' (Approved: Aug 2024)**
Risk Level: Medium | Review Cadence: Annual | DPA Status: Executed
Sub-processor list last updated: Jul 2024
Notes: Processes PII (email, name, org) for integration sync feature. SOC2 Type II report received Aug 2024 (reporting period Jan-Jun 2024).

**Google Drive — /Legal/DPAs/Executed/CloudSync_DPA_v2.pdf (signed Aug 12, 2024)**
Parties: Meridian Systems, Inc. ('Controller') and CloudSync Technologies, Inc. ('Processor')
Section 4.2: Processor shall not engage sub-processors without prior written consent.
Section 9.1: This agreement is binding upon successors and assigns, provided Controller receives 30-day written notice of any change of control.
Schedule B — Approved Sub-processors: AWS (us-east-1), Datadog, Twilio SendGrid

**Slack — #security-reviews (Mar 18, 2026, 2:44 PM)**
@priya.v: Heads up — got a notification from CloudSync that they've been acquired by Nextera Data Corp effective March 1. They're saying 'business as usual, same team, same infrastructure.'
@jake.morrison: Didn't we just renew with them? Is the DPA still good?
@priya.v: Checking. The DPA has a successor clause but requires 30-day prior notice. This was sent 18 days after close.
@jake.morrison: So they're technically in breach?
@priya.v: Potentially. But the real question is whether Nextera's own sub-processor list matches what we approved.
[Thread continues below]

**Slack — #security-reviews thread (Mar 18, 2026, 3:12 PM)**
@priya.v: Just pulled Nextera's SOC2 from their trust page. Their report lists GCP as primary infra, not AWS. If CloudSync migrates to parent infra, that's a new sub-processor we never approved.
@jake.morrison: :eyes:
@priya.v: Also — Nextera's own DPA template references their 'Nextera Data Corp' entity. Our DPA is with 'CloudSync Technologies Inc' which... might not exist anymore as a legal entity post-merger.
@engineering-leads: :thumbsup: (3 reactions)

**Jira — SEC-1142 (Created: Mar 19, 2026)**
Summary: Evaluate CloudSync/Nextera acquisition impact on data processing authorization
Status: In Progress | Priority: High
Comment (@priya.v, Mar 20): 'Confirmed with their legal: CloudSync Technologies Inc was dissolved into Nextera Data Corp on March 8. They are operating as a division, not a subsidiary. The legal entity party to our DPA no longer exists.'

**Confluence — Security Policy SP-007: Third-Party Data Processing (v3.2, last updated Jan 2026)**
Section 3.4: 'In the event a vendor undergoes change of control, all data processing activities under existing DPAs shall be suspended within 5 business days of security team awareness unless (a) successor entity executes a new DPA, or (b) existing DPA successor clause was properly invoked with required notice.'
[RESOLVED COMMENT by @priya.v, Nov 2025]: 'Added the 5-business-day suspension clause after the PartnerStack situation. VP Eng signed off in the Nov governance meeting.'
